    "whatItTellsYou": "The average daytime and nighttime levels diagnose hypertension more reliably than a single clinic reading and detect white-coat or masked hypertension. The size of the nocturnal dip carries prognostic weight: a blunted or absent dip (a non-dipper pattern) is linked to higher cardiovascular risk, and a steep early-morning surge is associated with strokes and heart attacks that cluster in the morning hours.",
